.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_REG_GENERATION 3 "2026-07-13" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_reg_generation \- caller-visible identity token for a registration's fd
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

uint64_t ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function returns a value that only grows and that is unique
across one loop. reg belongs to an fd, or to the bridge of a queue or
of a ccol_channel, and the library mints the value one time, when it
first registers that fd, on the new-entry path of
ccol_event_loop_add(). Every registration on that same fd shares the
value for as long as the fd lives. This includes a call to
ccol_event_loop_modify(), because a flip of the direction keeps the
same fd and the same connection below it, and it includes a read
registration and a write registration on the same fd, which share one
generation because they stand for one logical connection.

This value exists for the defensive bookkeeping of a caller across the
reuse of an fd. A caller that holds a connection object across several
asynchronous steps can stamp that object with the generation that it
reads right after ccol_event_loop_add() returns, and later compare
that stamp with a fresh read. The comparison shows whether the caller
reasons about the same logical connection, or whether something closed
the fd number and an unrelated new connection has taken it.

Basic correctness does not need the generation: the dispatch path of
ccol_event_loop always checks that a registration is live before it
calls any callback, whether or not a caller ever calls this function.
With more than one reactor thread, the single poller thread collects
readiness, and a separate dispatch worker thread runs the callback
after that. A registration can disappear between the collect step and
the process step of one batch, because something removes it or a
brand new registration reuses its fd; the loop then safely skips it at
dispatch time, never dispatches it wrongly, and never causes a
use-after-free. The return value of this function is only for the
bookkeeping of the application, on top of that guarantee.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  ccol_event_loop reg belongs to.
reg   Registration to query.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the generation value.
Returns 0 when reg is CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID, or when reg fails to
resolve for another reason, such as an earlier removal, and it also
returns 0 when loop is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, or a handle that is
stale or that something already destroyed. 0 is never a valid
generation for a real registration, because the counter starts at 1.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Safe to call at any time on any ccol_event_reg value that
ccol_event_loop_add ever returned for this loop, including a value
that something already removed, either earlier on this thread or on
another thread that races this call. The library resolves reg against
the registration table of loop before it dereferences anything, so it
always finds a handle that is stale, or that something already
removed, and reports generation 0; it never causes a use-after-free.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call from any thread, at any time, including at the same time
as a dispatch, as ccol_event_loop_modify(), or as
ccol_event_loop_remove().
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct conn {
    ccol_event_reg reg;
    uint64_t generation;
} conn_t;

conn_t *c = malloc(sizeof(*c));
if (!c) { perror("malloc"); exit(1); }
/* A callback of this registration can run before the next two stores,
   so it takes the handle from its reg parameter, never from c->reg. */
c->reg = ccol_event_loop_add(loop, ccol_selectable_from_fd(fd, ccol_select_read),
                        handlers, c, NULL);
c->generation = ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(loop, c->reg);

/* ... later, from another asynchronous step that holds a conn_t* ... */
if (ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(loop, c->reg) != c->generation) {
    /* stale: something closed this fd number and reused it */
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_modify (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_remove (3)
